# Git hooks and shell scripts MUST stay LF in the working tree. A CRLF in the
# shebang makes Git-for-Windows' sh fail with "bad interpreter: .../bash^M",
# silently disabling the hook. eol=lf overrides core.autocrlf on checkout.
.githooks/** text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf

# Node scripts must stay LF for the SAME reason, and the failure is quieter.
# vite/esbuild strip a shebang by matching `#!...\n`; with CRLF the stray \r
# survives and the module fails to parse — so `npm run test:scripts` died with
# "SyntaxError: Invalid or unexpected token" and ran ZERO tests, while
# scripts/verify.mjs still invoked it in the web lane.
#
# It only bites on Windows: the blobs are LF, core.autocrlf=true rewrites them
# to CRLF on checkout, and CI on Linux therefore stayed green the whole time. A
# suite that looks like coverage and runs nothing is worse than no suite, and
# nothing failed to signal it. eol=lf overrides core.autocrlf on checkout.
*.mjs text eol=lf
*.cjs text eol=lf

# The Gradle wrapper has no extension, so `*.sh` above never covered it. It also
# needs its EXECUTABLE BIT set in the index (100755) — it was committed 100644,
# which made `./gradlew assembleDebug` die with "Permission denied" (exit 126) as
# the FIRST step of Android CI. That lane therefore never once compiled the
# module: the job failed before Gradle started, on every push since it was added.
# Both halves matter, and the mode is not something .gitattributes can enforce —
# if you ever re-add this file, check `git ls-files -s android/gradlew` says
# 100755.
gradlew text eol=lf
